## 7. Traffic engine (shared by TX / RX / BD / PING / Combo)

This is the core reusable engine. Build it once, reuse for every traffic
direction.

### `ask_channel()` / `pcap_name()` / `compute_bw()` — see above.

### `_extract_interval(line)`
Regex-parse one iperf interval line:
`(\d+(\.\d+)?)\s*-\s*(\d+(\.\d+)?)\s+sec\s+(\d+(\.\d+)?)\s+MBytes\s+(\d+(\.\d+)?)\s+Mbits/sec`
Return `(start_sec, end_sec, mbytes, mbps)` floats or `None` if no match.

### `server_throughput(filepath)`
Parse ALL interval lines in a server log. Prefer the "summary" line — the
interval whose `start≈min(all starts)` and `end≈max(all ends)` and whose
duration exceeds 1.5s (this is iperf's final cumulative summary row, not
a per-second interval). If found, return `(mbps, "summary")`. Else average
all per-interval mbps values and return `(avg, "average")`. If file
missing → `(None, "missing")`. If no parseable lines → `(None, "no data")`.

### `bd_throughput(filepath)`
Bidirectional variant: only look at lines BEFORE the `"Server Report"`
marker (BD iperf logs both directions; only the pre-marker section is the
direction we're measuring here). Prefer a `[SUM-2]` line whose interval
starts <0.5s and ends >55s (full-duration summary). Else average all
`[SUM-2]` interval lines. Method strings: `"SUM-2"` / `"SUM-2-avg"` /
`"missing"` / `"no SUM-2"`.

### `ping_rtt(filepath)`
Regex `rtt min/avg/max/mdev = [\d.]+/([\d.]+)/` on any line, return the
avg as float, or `None`.

### `rssi_mid(filepath, duration)`
RSSI logs have ~1 sample per second. Return the line nearest the midpoint
second (`max(1, int(duration)//2)`, 1-based → clamp to last line if short),
stripped of any leading `"N: "` numeric prefix. `None` if file missing/empty.

### `count_zero_intervals(filepath)`
Count interval lines whose parsed mbps is EXACTLY `0.0`. **Bug to avoid**:
do NOT substring-match `"0.00 Mbits/sec"` against raw text (it would also
match inside `"10.00 Mbits/sec"`) — always go through `_extract_interval`
and compare the parsed float, never raw substring search, for this
specific check.

### `detect_stalls(clients)`
For each client index, check both its `s{i+1}.txt` (server) and
`c{i+1}.txt` (client) logs via `count_zero_intervals`. Collect
`(STA_label, ip, role, filename, zero_count)` for every log with ≥1 zero
interval.

### `extract_iperf_cmd(script_file, ttype)`
Pull the resolved iperf command line for a given TYPE out of a local
client/server shell script, by scanning lines containing both `"iperf"`
and `"tee"`, then disambiguating tcp (`no -u, no -S 0x`) vs udp (`-u`, no
`-S 0x`) vs vi (`-S 0xA0`) vs vo (`-S 0xE0`).

### `final_iperf_cmds(ttype, dut, clients, server_side, client_script, server_script)`
For documentation purposes in the final summary, reconstruct the EXACT
iperf commands actually run per-STA by taking the raw template line
(via `extract_iperf_cmd`) and substituting `$SERVER_IP`, `$PORT`,
`$DURATION`, `$OUTPUT_FILE` with the real runtime values. `server_side`
determines whether the server ran on the client or the DUT (affects which
host/IP goes where).

### `start_ping(dut, clients, direction, prefix)`
`direction`: `"d2c"` = DUT pings each client; `"c2d"` = each client pings
DUT. For each client, build `./ping.sh <target_ip> <prefix>_{i}.txt` and
launch it via `execute_command(..., new_terminal=True, terminal_name=
f"{prefix}_STA{i+1}")` — **non-blocking, opens one terminal per client, all
run in parallel**. Return the list of issued command strings (for logging).

### `fetch_ping(dut, clients, direction, prefix)`
After sleeping enough for the pings to finish (`ping -c 10` ≈ 10s, so
callers always `time.sleep(13)` between `start_ping` and `fetch_ping` —
10s + 3s buffer, because `start_ping` returns immediately and does NOT
wait for the terminal processes), scp each `{prefix}_{i}.txt` back from
whichever host (dut or client) actually ran it.

### `gather_throughput(clients, is_bd)` / `gather_ping(clients, prefix)`
Thin wrappers that loop clients and call the parsing functions above,
building row tuples `(idx, ip, type, value, method, rssi)` /
`(idx, ip, type, rtt)`.

### `_next_iteration(xlsx_path="wlan_results.xlsx")`
Open the workbook (or return 1 if it doesn't exist / openpyxl missing),
scan every row's first cell for a string starting with `"Iteration"`,
parse the trailing integer, return `max+1`. This is how iteration numbers
auto-increment across repeated runs into the same shared workbook.

### `_fmt(v, nd)`
`f"%.{nd}f" % v` if not None else `"NO DATA"`. Used everywhere numeric
values are written into text summaries.

